#!/usr/bin/env bash

lord --verbose << "EOF"
Now do a second pass focused specifically on **mirroring the visual and semantic primitives defined in `master.tex`**.

The EPUB renderer should not merely preserve the text content. It should understand the **design system encoded by the LaTeX macros** and reproduce that design system in semantic XHTML + CSS as faithfully as makes sense in a reflowable ebook.

## Read `master.tex` first

Before changing the ebook styling further, carefully inspect `master.tex`.

Do not skim it only for obvious font definitions.

Find and understand the definitions of the major authoring/layout primitives, especially:

```tex
\Verse
\Table
\Def
```

and all related macros used by those primitives.

Also inspect things such as:

```tex
\aA
\aB
\aC

\fA
\fB
\fC

\eJ
\hJ
\paleo
\egypt
```

and any other custom commands/environments that materially affect the book's appearance or semantic structure.

Search recursively for helper macros called by these primitives.

The goal is to understand not merely what their arguments contain, but **what visual relationship the LaTeX implementation is trying to create**.

For each major primitive, answer internally:

* What does this construct mean?
* What arguments does it take?
* Which arguments are content versus styling/layout control?
* How does it arrange those arguments?
* Which fonts does it use?
* Which colors?
* Which borders?
* Which spacing?
* Which alignment?
* Which text size?
* Which directionality?
* Which material is visually primary?
* Which material is commentary or annotation?
* Which elements are intentionally aligned with one another?
* How does it behave in narrow or alternate layouts?
* Are there already notions of horizontal versus vertical layout?

Then implement the corresponding EPUB component.

## `\Verse{}` is especially important

Treat `\Verse{}` as one of the core layout primitives of the ebook.

Read its **actual definition in `master.tex`**, including every helper it calls.

Do not infer its design from its name.

Understand how it represents the relationship among:

* verse number
* original-language text
* translation
* commentary
* source coloring
* footnotes
* annotations
* any alternate readings or special material

The EPUB version should default to the **vertical mode** of the verse design.

There is already a vertical-mode concept or implementation in `master.tex`; inspect that definition for the exact intended visual relationship.

Use that as the conceptual basis for the ebook layout.

In other words, for EPUB, prefer something conceptually like:

```text
VERSE NUMBER

ORIGINAL TEXT

TRANSLATION

COMMENTARY
```

rather than trying to preserve a wide paper-oriented side-by-side layout.

But do not implement that crude sketch literally without first reading the real LaTeX definition.

The actual vertical-mode implementation in `master.tex` is the specification.

Translate its design into CSS.

The EPUB version may simplify paper-specific geometry, but it should preserve:

* hierarchy;
* spacing relationships;
* font distinctions;
* source coloring;
* alignment logic;
* indentation;
* borders/backgrounds if present;
* transitions between verse text and commentary;
* treatment of verse numbers;
* treatment of footnotes;
* RTL/LTR behavior.

If `\Verse{}` has multiple layout modes, the ebook should generally choose the one closest to the LaTeX **vertical mode**, because that maps naturally onto narrow reflowable screens.

## Build an EPUB component corresponding to `\Verse`

Do not generate ad hoc inline styles for every verse.

Create a reusable semantic structure and centralized CSS.

For example, the resulting XHTML might conceptually have classes such as:

```html
<section class="verse">
    <div class="verse-number">...</div>

    <div class="verse-source">
        ...
    </div>

    <div class="verse-translation">
        ...
    </div>

    <div class="verse-commentary">
        ...
    </div>
</section>
```

This is illustrative.

Derive the final component hierarchy from what `\Verse{}` actually does.

If the LaTeX implementation has meaningful nested regions, preserve them as nested semantic elements rather than flattening them.

## Mirror `\Table{}`

Read the full definition of:

```tex
\Table{...}{...}
```

including any helpers it calls.

Determine what stylistic conventions distinguish a `\Table` from an ordinary LaTeX `tabular`.

Things to inspect include:

* header typography;
* horizontal rules;
* vertical rules;
* row spacing;
* cell padding;
* text alignment;
* numeric alignment;
* alternating treatments, if any;
* font size;
* color;
* captions;
* wrapping behavior;
* special handling of RTL content;
* surrounding vertical spacing.

Reproduce the **design language** of `\Table` in HTML tables and CSS.

Use semantic HTML:

```html
<table class="wtn-table">
    ...
</table>
```

with:

```html
<thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th>
<td>
```

where appropriate.

Do not merely emit a browser-default table.

The EPUB table should visually look recognizably like the print `\Table`, adjusted for small screens and reflow.

Where a print table is too wide, preserve its semantics using wrapping or an ebook-specific stacked adaptation rather than making the text microscopic.

## Mirror `\Def{}`

Read the actual definition of:

```tex
\Def
```

and every helper it uses.

Determine exactly how definitions are visually presented.

Look for:

* the treatment of the defined term;
* typography of the headword;
* indentation;
* spacing;
* borders or rules;
* background;
* line spacing;
* examples;
* nested `\aA`, `\aB`, `\aC`, etc.;
* language-specific fonts;
* lexical annotations;
* pronunciation/transliteration;
* footnotes.

Create a reusable EPUB definition component, e.g. conceptually:

```html
<section class="definition">
    <div class="definition-term">...</div>
    <div class="definition-body">...</div>
</section>
```

Again, derive the actual structure from `master.tex`.

The ebook result should feel like the same `\Def` component rendered in a different medium.

## Mirror the whole macro design system

Do this not only for the three named primitives.

Search `master.tex` for custom commands that establish recurring visual patterns.

Build a mapping such as:

```text
LaTeX primitive
    ↓
semantic EPUB component
    ↓
CSS class/system
```

Examples might end up like:

```text
\Verse        → .verse
\Table        → .wtn-table
\Def          → .definition
\aA           → .annotation-a
\aB           → .annotation-b
\aC           → .annotation-c
\fA           → EPUB footnote class/type A
\fB           → EPUB footnote class/type B
\fC           → EPUB footnote class/type C
\hJ           → Hebrew/source-language component
\eJ           → translation component
\paleo        → Paleo-Hebrew span
\egypt        → Egyptian hieroglyph span
```

But do not assume those semantics until you read their definitions.

Some macros may primarily exist for visual styling; others may encode actual editorial semantics.

Preserve that distinction.

## CSS should mirror LaTeX relationships, not literal dimensions

Do not mechanically convert:

```tex
\vspace{4mm}
```

into:

```css
margin-top: 4mm;
```

That is not the goal.

Instead determine what the spacing means.

For example:

* “small separation within a unit”
* “large separation between units”
* “headword hangs above body”
* “commentary is subordinate to translation”
* “verse blocks need visible breathing room”

Then express those relationships using robust relative CSS units:

```css
em
rem
%
```

and reusable spacing variables where practical.

Likewise, do not blindly translate TeX dimensions designed for a 7-inch printed page into fixed ebook dimensions.

## Preserve typography

Inspect all font definitions associated with these macros.

Where the print book intentionally distinguishes:

* serif versus sans;
* bold versus normal;
* actual small caps;
* italic;
* condensed or display fonts;
* Hebrew;
* Paleo-Hebrew;
* Egyptian;
* Syriac;
* IPA;
* special symbols;

attempt to preserve those distinctions.

If the font should be embedded, embed it.

If ordinary English prose should remain reader-selectable, preserve the distinction through relative styling rather than unnecessarily locking the entire book into a publisher font.

## Preserve color semantics

Read the actual color definitions in `master.tex`.

Do not guess based on screenshots if the exact color values are already defined in source.

Map meaningful book colors into centralized EPUB CSS variables where possible, such as conceptually:

```css
:root {
    --source-j: ...;
    --source-e: ...;
    --source-p: ...;
    --annotation-a: ...;
    --annotation-b: ...;
    --annotation-c: ...;
}
```

Adapt colors only where necessary for ebook contrast or Kindle dark mode.

If you change an ebook color from the print value, document why.

## Use the LaTeX source as the visual specification

Where there is ambiguity between:

* the current crude EPUB output;
* generic ebook conventions;
* and `master.tex`;

prefer `master.tex`.

The existing ebook is only a starting implementation.

The mature LaTeX renderer represents years of accumulated design decisions and should be treated as the **authoritative specification for the visual grammar of the book**.

The EPUB implementation is a translation of that grammar into another medium.

## Do not imitate TeX internals unnecessarily

You do not need to recreate:

* TeX boxes;
* glue;
* penalties;
* exact baseline calculations;
* absolute paper geometry;
* fixed page breaks;

unless their effect encodes an important visual relationship.

Translate the **visible intent**, not the implementation mechanism.

For example:

```text
TeX minipage
```

does not necessarily mean:

```text
HTML fixed-width box
```

It may simply mean:

```text
visually grouped semantic region
```

Likewise, a TeX table used purely to align two conceptual regions might be better represented by ordinary block layout in the EPUB.

## Make the mapping maintainable

Do not scatter knowledge of LaTeX primitives across unrelated scripts.

Create a coherent translation layer.

Ideally there should be an obvious place in the ebook code where one can understand:

```text
\Verse → verse renderer
\Def   → definition renderer
\Table → table renderer
...
```

If practical, add comments referencing the relevant macro definitions in `master.tex`.

Do not duplicate the LaTeX implementation line-for-line.

Instead document the semantic relationship.

## Create a CSS design system

After studying `master.tex`, reorganize the EPUB CSS so it has a recognizable design system.

For example, where appropriate:

```css
:root {
    --space-xs: ...;
    --space-sm: ...;
    --space-md: ...;
    --space-lg: ...;

    --verse-number-size: ...;
    --commentary-size: ...;

    --source-j: ...;
    --source-e: ...;
    --source-p: ...;
}
```

Use classes representing book concepts, not incidental visual properties.

Prefer:

```css
.verse-commentary
```

over:

```css
.small-gray-block
```

Prefer:

```css
.definition-term
```

over:

```css
.bold-heading-2
```

## Compare actual output

After implementing the mapping, pick representative examples from the book containing:

* a simple `\Verse`;
* a complicated `\Verse`;
* a verse with Hebrew;
* a verse with several source colors;
* a verse with multiple footnotes;
* a verse with a long commentary;
* a `\Def`;
* a complicated `\Def`;
* a small `\Table`;
* a wide `\Table`;
* mixed-script content;
* nested annotations.

Compare the print rendering to the EPUB rendering.

Do not require identical geometry.

Ask instead:

> Does the ebook immediately communicate the same hierarchy and editorial structure?

If not, keep adjusting the CSS/component structure.

## Document the primitive mapping

Add a section to `ebook/README.md` called something like:

```text
LaTeX → EPUB component mapping
```

Document the important mappings and any intentional differences.

For example:

```text
\Verse
Print:
    May use horizontal or vertical layouts depending on context.

EPUB:
    Always uses the vertical-style representation, based on the
    vertical implementation in master.tex, because it is robust
    under narrow reflow.

\Table
Print:
    ...

EPUB:
    ...
```

Do this for the most important primitives.

## Final principle

`master.tex` is not merely a print stylesheet.

It contains the project's **design vocabulary**.

Treat constructs like:

```tex
\Verse
\Table
\Def
```

the same way a web application would treat reusable UI components.

Your job is to build their EPUB/CSS equivalents.

The desired relationship is:

```text
same source concept
        ↓
LaTeX renderer      EPUB renderer
        ↓                 ↓
print-native       ebook-native
presentation       presentation
```

The two outputs should not have identical geometry.

They **should clearly have the same designer**.

For `\Verse`, specifically, use the **vertical mode as the default ebook interpretation**, and read the implementation in `master.tex` carefully before deciding what that means.

Also, don't center the text! Attempt to make it like the latex.
EOF
